<p>Port Arthur hail damage claims are less common than wind claims, but they can be just as costly. NOAA’s Storm Events Database records large hail in and around the city: 1.75-inch hail in Port Arthur on December 28, 2024 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1226827" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>), 2-inch hail in Groves on May 12, 2024 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1187394" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>), and 2.75-inch hail at Sabine Pass in April 2003. Because hail here arrives with fast-moving thunderstorm lines rather than every spring, many owners do not think to check their roofs afterward. The damage may only surface later as a leak, and by then the storm date is harder to prove.</p>

<h2>What Hail Does to Port Arthur Properties</h2>
<p>Hail damage depends on stone size, wind speed during the storm, and the age and type of material. Common damage includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Composition shingles:</strong> bruises, granule loss, and fractured mats that shorten the roof’s life</li>
<li><strong>Metal roofs and buildings:</strong> dents in panels, trim, and ridge caps; damaged sealant at fasteners</li>
<li><strong>Low-slope commercial roofs:</strong> punctured or cracked membranes and crushed insulation boards</li>
<li><strong>Soft metals:</strong> gutters, downspouts, vents, flashing, and AC condenser fins</li>
<li><strong>Windows and siding:</strong> broken glass, torn screens, cracked vinyl siding, and chipped paint</li>
<li><strong>Skylights and outdoor equipment:</strong> cracked domes, damaged pool and HVAC equipment</li>
</ul>
<p>For roof-specific issues such as repair vs. replacement and permits, see our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> page.</p>

