<h2>Proving the Hail Storm Date</h2>
<p>The most common fight in a hail claim is not whether hail hit the roof, but when. Insurers may argue the damage predates the policy, came from an earlier storm, or is simply wear. Evidence that can help tie damage to a specific date:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>NOAA / NWS storm reports</td><td>Hail size and location on a specific date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Photos of hail on the ground</td><td>Stone size and timing, especially with a coin or ruler for scale</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh marks on soft metals</td><td>Bright, unoxidized dents suggest recent impact</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prior roof photos or inspections</td><td>Condition before the storm (real estate listings and appraisals sometimes help)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neighbor claims and repairs</td><td>Pattern of damage across the street or subdivision</td></tr>
<tr><td>Radar and storm data</td><td>Path of the hail core over the property</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> tool and <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page draw on NOAA data for Jefferson County.</p>

