<h2>Policy Terms That Shape a Hail Settlement</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Who covers hail.</strong> If your homeowners policy excludes wind and hail, hail damage is a <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA claim</a>, with TWIA’s own deadlines.</li>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductible.</strong> Often a percentage of the dwelling limit. A 1% deductible on a $200,000 home is $2,000; a 2% deductible is $4,000.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusions.</strong> Common on metal roofs; they remove coverage for dents that do not affect function.</li>
<li><strong>ACV roof coverage or payment schedules.</strong> Older roofs may be paid at depreciated value only.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Whether undamaged sections must be replaced so materials match varies by policy and facts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where Hail Estimates Fall Short</h2>
<p>Hail estimates are often written quickly after a storm. Items we frequently see missing or underpriced include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Soft metals such as gutters, downspouts, and vents</li>
<li>AC condenser fin damage</li>
<li>Window screens and wrapped trim</li>
<li>Painting of hail-spattered siding or fences</li>
<li>Roof accessories, drip edge, and code-required items</li>
<li>Interior water damage from roof punctures</li>
</ul>
<p>An <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can compare the insurer’s scope with the property. If the claim was denied, our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page explains how to ask for a written explanation and what to do next.</p>

<h2>What to Do in the First Week After a Hail Storm</h2>
<p>Hail damage is easy to put off because the house usually still looks fine from the street. A few steps taken right away make the claim much easier to support later.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Photograph the hail itself.</strong> If stones are still on the ground, take pictures next to a coin, ruler, or tape measure. Note the time.</li>
<li><strong>Walk the property.</strong> Check gutters, downspouts, window screens, the AC condenser, painted wood, fences, outdoor furniture, and vehicles. Fresh dents on soft metals are some of the best evidence you will have.</li>
<li><strong>Look inside.</strong> Check ceilings below the roof and around skylights for new stains over the next few rains.</li>
<li><strong>Write down the date.</strong> Hail storms around Port Arthur often come with severe wind, and knowing the exact date helps later when you match damage to NOAA records.</li>
<li><strong>Get a close roof inspection.</strong> A ground-level look rarely shows bruising or mat fractures. Avoid going on the roof yourself.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> to the right insurer, whether that is your homeowners carrier or TWIA.</li>
<li><strong>Be cautious with door-to-door offers.</strong> After a hail storm, contractors may canvass neighborhoods. You can get repair bids, but your insurer and your policy decide the claim.</li>
</ol>
<p>Owners of commercial and industrial buildings should also check rooftop units, skylights, and the membrane around drains and penetrations, where hail punctures often lead to leaks months later. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a printable version.</p>

